{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"reasoning","text":"Now student tests. Need import assert/test and functions. Let's craft two or more tests covering edge cases. We can include tests about:\n- ineligible runs with invalid fields are ignored and valid runs still produce summary.\n- tie-breaking by run_id when score and elapsed equal.\n- render validation throws TypeError.\n- median averaging for even count.\n- summarizeAssistants throws TypeError on non-array and on invalid eligible run.\nMake at least two meaningful. Let's add maybe 4 tests. Ensure not modifying committed suite. Use node:test.\n\nNeed decide test data. Ensure summarizeAssistants median for Codex in committed test: elapsed times 1000 and 2000 median 1500. Our code uses numeric sort: [1000,2000], mid=1, even -> (1000+2000)/2=1500. Good.\n\nPotential issue: sort by assistant: (a.assistant < b.assistant ? -1 : 1). If equal returns 1, which is okay because values unique keys. But could cause non-stable? No duplicates. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: comparison_eligible false is an explicit false. Using === false. But if run.comparison_eligible is undefined => not false, eligible. Good.\n\nPotential issue: use optional chaining `run?.status` could be undefined if run null/undefined. Then not equal complete -> ineligible, no validation. That's correct because ineligible invalid fields don't matter. But if run is primitive? run?.status returns undefined for null/undefined, but for number? Optional chaining on non-object throws? Actually `null?.x` works; `undefined?.x` works; `5?.x` throws TypeError? Optional chaining expects object, not primitive. In JS `5?.status` => TypeError: Cannot read properties of number (reading 'status')? Wait optional chaining: `nullish` short-circuits; non-nullish non-object throws. The test uses frozen objects. But contract says runs is array of objects. But if array contains number? Should probably throw TypeError due to validation? Hmm. The contract says when runs is not an array throw TypeError. It doesn't specify if array elements are non-objects. Could ignore. But our code may throw TypeError from optional chaining on number? Actually optional chaining requires the base to be nullish or object. For primitives, throws TypeError. Is that acceptable? The contract doesn't define; tests likely only objects. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: summary.best_score toString: if value is 95.0 string is \"95\". The render test uses 95.5. No test for 95.0. Probably okay.\n\nNeed ensure Number.isInteger for run_count works when it's a Number object? Not needed.\n\nEdge case: median_elapsed_ms finite negative? validation prevents. Good.\n\nNow write tests."}}
